data srv4_memory_population_observation_note: String = "srv4 BMC 192.168.1.195 Redfish observation 2026-08-22, taken between 19:46Z and 20:15Z (the exact minute was not recorded at read time and is NOT reconstructed here -- the window is bounded below by the 19:45:57Z correctable-ECC entry this session read first and above by the srv3 read at 20:15:38Z). srv3 carries a precise timestamp because its read was taken against a live boot transition and the clock mattered; this one was not, and a fabricated second would be worth less than an honest window. Taken through the rotated credential in Secret Manager (bmc-srv4-admin) -- the authenticated read this host's deferral named as its dissolution condition, so that scaffold is RETIRED by this row rather than carried forward. MemorySummary TotalSystemMemoryGiB=512; per-DIMM dimm0..dimm7 ALL M393A8G40D40-CRB CapacityMiB=65536 OperatingSpeedMhz=2133 RankCount=2 Manufacturer=Samsung, eight distinct serials, banks DDR4_A1 through H1, every module State=Enabled Health=OK and system HealthRollup=OK.\n\nTHE DEFERRAL'S OWN INSTRUCTION SAID TO DECODE AGAINST THE SK HYNIX CATALOG AND THAT INSTRUCTION IS SUPERSEDED, which is worth saying because following it literally would have produced a wrong row. It was written when the expected finding was srv1's uniform Hynix population; what is actually installed is a Samsung part that had no catalog row at all until 2026-08-22. The deferral was right that srv1's population must not be assumed, and wrong about which catalog the answer would come from.\n\nONE CORRECTABLE ECC EVENT IS ON RECORD FOR THIS POPULATION, logged 2026-08-22T19:45:57Z on Socket 0 / Channel 0 / DIMM 0 (bank 13, row 166, column 96), about three minutes after an unclean restart following a power blackout. It was corrected by ECC and no uncorrectable event accompanied it. It is recorded here rather than left in the BMC log because it is the only memory event this population has produced and because a repeat at the same bank/row/column would distinguish a marginal device from a single upset -- see gunbc.host.host_memory_qualification for the qualification standing this does not by itself change."

data srv4_memory_population: HostMemoryPopulation = HostMemoryPopulation {
  rows: [
    MemoryPopulationRow { module: m393a8g40d40_crb0q_catalog, stick_count: 8 },
  ],
}

data srv4_host: ComputeHost = ComputeHost {
  identity: operator_host_srv4,
  baseboard: Present { value: srv4_baseboard },
  bmc_intent: Present { value: fleet_bmc_valid_state },
  processors: [CpuProcessor { cpu: fleet_intent_cpu }],
  memory: memory_devices_from_population(pop: srv4_memory_population),
  storage: [srv4_nvme0],
  network_interfaces: [],
}

// SUPERMICRO H11SSL UNIT 3 (host-e2d4b7): the third H11SSL-i in the fleet, and the only one whose operating
// system was put on by HAND at srv4's PXE edge. This row is what that hand install never produced: the
// fleet's own statement of the machine it intends to supply, so the converge -- not the person who installed
// it -- holds the box from here.
//
// The board is stated at FAMILY grain on the operator's 2026-11-15 report. No `dmidecode -t baseboard` read
// of THIS board is on record, which is why its machine standing in gunbc.fleet.machine_board is
// identity_stated_board and why a serial is absent here rather than borrowed from its siblings.
//
// The BMC address is the operator's (192.168.1.249 -- before this row that address existed in the corpus
// only as a test literal in gunbc.machine_intake.bmc_recovery_route). Its PROTOCOL is not a preference:
// extdeps.bmc.supermicro::supermicro_supported_protocols_floor lists Ipmi ALONE, because on this family
// Redfish is licence-gated and an unlicensed service root refuses with OemLicenseNotPassed; the receipts
// behind that floor are the two sibling H11SSL units in gunbc.machine_intake.supermicro_bmc_reading, which
// answered IPMI Get Device ID with manufacturer 10876. NO address or protocol read has been taken against
// .249 itself. The read that closes it is `ipmitool mc info` IN BAND over the host's local KCS interface, or
// over LAN only after the credential rotation -- never with the factory credential the box still uses. That
// rotation is modeled as pending (RotationRouteNotYetRunnable in
// gunbc.machine_intake.supermicro_h11ssl_unit3_intake_observation), so what this row carries is the fleet's
// intent for the control plane, not a control plane this repository has established.
data supermicro_h11ssl_unit3_baseboard: ServerBaseboard = ServerBaseboard {
  catalog: supermicro_h11ssl_catalog,
  bmc: BmcEndpoint { host: "192.168.1.249", protocol: Ipmi },
}

// THE SAME HONESTY ABOUT WHAT HAS NEVER BEEN READ. No CPU census, no memory census and no storage census
// exists for this box: the hand install left no receipt, and the fleet SSH roster was never seeded on it, so
// those reads are OWED, not skipped -- the missing roster is itself a register row
// (gunbc.integration_concern::h11ssl_unit3_hand_install_route_seeded_no_fleet_authorized_keys_roster).
// Empty lists state that standing, and the cost is paid where it lands: the host's committed runner width
// derives to unresolved rather than invented, exactly as mtcollins1's offer shows width arriving from a
// measured HostUsableRam read rather than from DIMM nominal capacity this box has never given us.
// Its install NIC MAC is on record in the intake module, but the ADDRESS standing there is a stale neighbour
// entry whose address is held in this module only by the dated observation
// fleet_intent_network::ken_host_lan_endpoint, which reserves nothing; no NetworkInterface is minted across an
// unconfirmed address, and the read that settles it is named on that standing row.
data supermicro_h11ssl_unit3_host: ComputeHost = ComputeHost {
  identity: operator_host_e2d4b7,
  baseboard: Present { value: supermicro_h11ssl_unit3_baseboard },
  bmc_intent: Present { value: fleet_bmc_valid_state },
  processors: [],
  memory: [],
  storage: [],
  network_interfaces: [],
}

data srv14_host: ComputeHost = ComputeHost {
  identity: operator_host_srv14,
  baseboard: Present { value: srv14_baseboard },
  bmc_intent: Present { value: fleet_bmc_valid_state },
  processors: [CpuProcessor { cpu: srv14_cpu }],
  memory: srv14_memory_devices,
  storage: [srv14_nvme0],
  network_interfaces: [],
}

// srv14/butch: an ALTRAD8UD-1L2T (same board family as srv1-4/srv13/srv15), read on butch over the
// fleet-automation key 2026-11-10 (ssh ubuntu@192.168.1.205). Board serial BR80S6102300077; BMC 192.168.1.250
// (MAC 9c:6b:00:e7:c9:fa); host NIC enP2p4s0 9c:6b:00:e7:ca:9c at 192.168.1.205/24 (DHCP -- a router DHCP
// reservation is an OPERATOR-GATED step, named in the PR). Installed 2026-10-30 from ubuntu-24.04.3-live-
// server-arm64 over the BMC's USB virtual-media gadget (installer block device "Linux File-Stor_Gadget",
// ds=nocloud;s=/cdrom/nocloud/) -- so its fleet backing is the pairing written by
// provisioning_over_bmc_via_virtual_media() (control Bmc, delivery InstalledViaVirtualMedia), NOT PXE; no new
// delivery variant is needed. The pairing has no literal form: OwnedProvisioning is sole_constructor at
// gunbc.fleet.fleet_host_backing, and the route-named constructors are the only way to write one.
data srv14_baseboard: ServerBaseboard = ServerBaseboard {
  catalog: asrock_altrad8ud_1l2t_catalog,
  bmc: BmcEndpoint { host: "192.168.1.250", protocol: Redfish },
}

// butch's CPU is a Neoverse-N1 AMPERE ALTRA Q80-30 (lscpu 2026-11-10: Model name Neoverse-N1, 80 CPUs,
// CPU max MHz 3000.0000, 1 NUMA node 0-79) -- the extdeps.cpu.ampere q8030 catalog, NOT the 128-core M128-30
// that fleet_intent_cpu carries for srv1-4. The deployment sustained-per-thread figure (3.0 GHz) is the
// catalog's own nominal_sustained_per_thread_hz and matches the observed CPU max MHz; butch is idle, so no
// distinct under-load clock has been measured, and using the catalog nominal is the same choice srv1-4 made.
data srv14_cpu: CpuFacts = CpuFacts {
  catalog: altra_q8030_catalog,
  deployment: CpuDeploymentFacts { sustained_per_thread_hz: hertz(3000000000) },
}

// butch's installed memory -- a HOST OBSERVATION, kept in the consuming (fleet) layer, not in the
// vendor extdeps catalog (design_argument external_upstreams: an observation this repository makes is a
// receipt in the observing layer, never a fact of the upstream). Read on butch 2026-11-10 by
// `sudo dmidecode -t 17`: four Samsung M393A8K40B22-CWD 64 GiB DDR4-2666 RDIMMs (Registered/Buffered,
// on-die ECC, SPD-reported rank 2) in DDR4_A1, B1, E1, F1 -- serials 40CE1048 / 40CE0C2B / 40CFFBAA /
// 40CE0D1F; DDR4_C1, D1, G1, H1 report No Module. That is 4 x 64 GiB = 256 GiB installed.
//
// ComputeHost.memory needs only per-device capacity/kind for RAM sizing (MemoryDevice), so the read is
// carried directly as four observed DDR4 devices -- NO vendor catalog row is authored from it, and NO
// unobserved organization (chip width, die stacking, logical rank) is asserted, because dmidecode does
// not report those and several organizations are consistent with what it does report. A faithful vendor
// catalog row for this part, and a proper per-DIMM census receipt (the machine_intake census pattern),
// are DISSOLVE-ON items for a follow-up that reads the Samsung datasheet or a deeper census; until then
// butch's memory is stated at exactly the granularity it was observed. The kernel's MemTotal 262937520 kB
// (~250.7 GiB usable) is a separate observation and is what the CI usable budget derives from (added in
// the runner-pool lane), NOT this nominal figure; the nominal-vs-usable gap is that firmware/kernel
// reservation (fleet_host_memory_nominal_vs_usable_note).
data srv14_memory_devices: List<MemoryDevice> = [
  MemoryDevice { capacity: byte_size(68719476736), memory_kind: Dram, bandwidth: none },
  MemoryDevice { capacity: byte_size(68719476736), memory_kind: Dram, bandwidth: none },
  MemoryDevice { capacity: byte_size(68719476736), memory_kind: Dram, bandwidth: none },
  MemoryDevice { capacity: byte_size(68719476736), memory_kind: Dram, bandwidth: none },
]

// butch's single boot disk, read on butch 2026-11-10 (`lsblk -b -d -o NAME,MODEL,SIZE /dev/nvme0n1`):
// SAMSUNG MZVPV512HDGL-000H1, 512110190592 bytes, on which / is LVM (ubuntu-vg, 466G, 198G used at read
// time). nvme-cli is NOT installed on butch, so the drive serial and firmware were not read; the model
// string and byte capacity are the lsblk read. This is an OEM drive with no brochure catalog row, so it is
// a BareDevice carrying only what was read: capacity + medium Nvme + persistence, with serial and read/
// write bandwidth UNMEASURED (Absent) rather than guessed from a similar part.
data srv14_nvme0: StorageDevice = BareDevice {
  capacity: byte_size(512110190592),
  medium: Nvme,
  serial: Absent,
  read_bandwidth: Absent,
  write_bandwidth: Absent,
  persistence: Persistent,
}

data fleet_intent_known_hosts: List<ComputeHost> = [srv1_host, srv2_host, srv3_host, srv4_host, srv14_host]

// WHAT THE FLEET ACTUALLY RUNS, WHICH CARRIES ONE FACT MORE THAN "Docker" DID.
//
// The old surface said `Docker` and stopped. Whether that daemon runs as root is the property that
// decides whether a job that escapes its container owns the machine, and the enum could not say it,
// so the surface was silent on the only security-relevant thing about the engine. srv1 runs the
// rootless daemon.
//
// A mode the product does not support yields no deployment at all rather than a surface asserting
// one, and a target with no engine refuses every image (product.fabric.customer_image's
// TargetHasNoContainerEngine) instead of admitting one it cannot run.
fn fleet_container_engine_deployment() -> ContainerEngineDeployment? {
  match container_engine_deployment(engine: docker_engine, privilege_mode: RootlessDaemon) {
    ContainerEngineDeployed { deployment: deployment } => Present { value: deployment }
    ContainerEngineDeploymentRefused { refusal: _ } => none
  }
}

fn fleet_intent_execution_surface() -> ExecutionSurface {
  ExecutionSurface {
    os: fleet_intent_os_surface,
    isolation: PerJobFilesystem,
    container_engine: fleet_container_engine_deployment(),
    toolchains: [sccache_toolchain_capability],
    mounted_storage: [],
    network: [],
  }
}
